{"name":"Mole","hex":"#392d2b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mole","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river in Surrey, England, tributary to the Thames.","A river in Devon, England, tributary to the Taw."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mole"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mole (animal)","blocks":[{"text":"Moles are small, subterranean mammals. They have cylindrical bodies, velvety fur, very small, inconspicuous eyes and ears, reduced hindlimbs, and short, powerful forelimbs with large paws adapted for digging."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Kret_mole.jpg/330px-Kret_mole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mole_(animal)","wikidata":"Q3189180"},{"title":"Talpidae","blocks":[{"text":"The family Talpidae includes the true moles, who are small insectivorous mammals of the order Eulipotyphla. Talpids are all digging animals to various degrees: moles are completely subterranean animals; shrew moles and shrew-like moles somewhat less so; and desmans, while basically aquatic, excavate dry sleeping chambers; whilst the quite unique star-nosed mole is equally adept in the water and underground. Talpids are found across the Northern Hemisphere of Eurasia and North America, and range as far south as the montane regions of tropical Southeast Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Talpidae_family_pictures.png/330px-Talpidae_family_pictures.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talpidae","wikidata":"Q104825"},{"title":"Mole (sauce)","blocks":[{"text":"Mole or mōlli is a traditional sauce and marinade originally used in Mexican cuisine. In contemporary Mexico, the term is used for numerous sauces, including some fifty distinct varieties of mole across Mexico. Generally, a mole sauce contains fruits, nuts, chili peppers, and spices like black pepper, cinnamon, or cumin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/ChickRedMole.JPG/330px-ChickRedMole.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mole_(sauce)","wikidata":"Q1495652"},{"title":"Mole (unit)","blocks":[{"text":"The mole (symbol mol) is a unit of measurement, the base unit in the International System of Units (SI) for amount of substance. One mole is an aggregate of exactly 6.02214076×1023 elementary entities which can be atoms, molecules, ions, ion pairs, or other particles. This number of entities equals 602,214,076,000,000,000,000,000, approximately 602 sextillion or 602 billion multiplied by one trillion. The number of particles in a mole is the Avogadro number (symbol N0) and the numerical value of the Avogadro constant (symbol NA) has units of mol−1. The relationship between the mole, Avogadro number, and Avogadro constant can be expressed in the following equation:The current SI value of the mole is based on the historical definition of the mole as the amount of substance that corresponds to the number of atoms in 12 grams of 12C, which made the molar mass of a compound in grams per mole, numerically equal to the average molecular mass or formula mass of the compound expressed in daltons. With the 2019 revision of the SI, the numerical equivalence is now only approximate, but may still be assumed with high accuracy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Mole_carbon-12_diagram.svg/330px-Mole_carbon-12_diagram.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mole_(unit)","wikidata":"Q41509"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Talpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg/500px-Talpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Talpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg/1280px-Talpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Talpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg/500px-Talpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mole paw","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATalpa_europaea_MHNT_pate.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Uropsilus1sm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Uropsilus1sm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Uropsilus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUropsilus1sm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Scapanus_latimanus2.jpg/500px-Scapanus_latimanus2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Scapanus_latimanus2.jpg/1280px-Scapanus_latimanus2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Scapanus_latimanus2.jpg/500px-Scapanus_latimanus2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Northern broad-footed mole","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AScapanus_latimanus2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Notoryctes_typhlops.jpg/500px-Notoryctes_typhlops.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Notoryctes_typhlops.jpg/1280px-Notoryctes_typhlops.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Notoryctes_